  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 237 AM MDT Sun Sep 20 2026\n\nRain continues across portions of the area, mainly central \nWyoming, as low pressure continues to move through the area. It \nis now fairly close to our office just after midnight with \nmoisture wrapping back into the Wind River Basin. Some areas had\na lot of rain yesterday afternoon and evening, with some street\nflooding in Lander. Most of the convective rain is done now, \nand is now mainly of the stratiform variety. The main area of \nrain will continue to move slowly east through the night and \ninto tomorrow with gradual drying as the low tracks to the east.\nWe will still have the chance for some flooding today as well, \nespecially later in the day if there is some destabilization of \nthe atmosphere to get some thunderstorms to fire. However, the \nimpacted area will be more restricted. Precipitable water values\nremain over 150 percent of normal along and east of the \nBighorns. So, we will maintain the Flood Watch here for the rest\nof the day. Across the western zones, with the deeper moisture \nmoving away, we will cancel the Watch with issuance this \nmorning. There will be enough moisture for isolated showers and \nstorms to fire back into the basins east of the Continental \nDivide and the adjacent mountains, but coverage looks rather \nsparse (generally less than a 1 out of 3 chance). Showers should\ngradually end this evening across the eastern zones with all \nareas dry by midnight. Some locations in Johnson and Natrona \nCounties could pick up 0.75 to 1.50 inches of additional rain \ntoday, especially where storms persist. However, not all areas \nwill see heavy rain.\n\nOnce we get past this evening, it looks like we will enter a \nquieter pattern. Monday looks like a nice last day of \nastronomical summer for 95 percent of the area with near normal \ntemperatures and a sunny to partly cloudy sky. There may be just\nenough moisture leftover for an isolated, and this means \nisolated with a capital I, shower or thunderstorm in and near \nthe mountains east of the Divide. The chance is at most 1 out of\n6 though, and nothing strong is expected. The only fly in the \nointment is an upper-level low moving northeastward from the \nDesert Southwest that will open into a trough and pass through \nTuesday afternoon through Wednesday morning. The GFS has trended\ntoward the ECMWF, so the chance of showers and maybe a few \nthunderstorms looks better. This system is a fast mover so no \nheavy rain or flooding is expected out of it at this time. Any \nprecipitation from this system should clear the area by sunset \non Wednesday. Following that, ridging should build across the \narea and bring dry weather for the rest of the work week and \npossibly into the start of next weekend. And with generally \nsouthwest flow across the area, temperatures should average \nabove normal, in some cases well above normal. Some of the \nwarmer spots may see highs in the middle or even the upper 80s \nfrom Thursday through Saturday.",
